/-!
# Failure of strictness for the Koszul and adic systems of quotients

Let `A` be a commutative ring and `f : Fin r → A` a finite sequence generating the ideal
`I = (f₀, …, f_{r-1})`. Two inverse systems of `A`-modules accompany it: the system
`A/(f₀ⁿ, …, f_{r-1}ⁿ)` of quotients by the ideals generated by the `n`-th powers, which is the
zeroth cohomology of the Koszul complex `Kₙ` on `f₀ⁿ, …, f_{r-1}ⁿ`, and the adic system `A/Iⁿ`.
They are isomorphic as pro-objects, because the two descending chains of ideals interleave:
`(f₀ⁿ, …, f_{r-1}ⁿ) ⊆ Iⁿ` always, and some power of `I` is contained in `(f₀ⁿ, …, f_{r-1}ⁿ)`.
The interleaving is cofinal but not uniform: the power of `I` one must take grows faster than
by a constant shift.

This file proves that the growth is genuinely unavoidable, so the pro-isomorphism is not
strict. A strict pro-morphism carries a single shift `c` valid at every level, and the strict
pro-category of `Stacks.LocalCohomology.StrictProCategory` is built from such shifted families.
The obstruction is extracted from one half of a strict pro-isomorphism: as soon as the
composite of a strict pro-morphism `A/I^• ⟶ A/(f^•)` with a strict pro-morphism back is
eventually the identity, the level-`n` component of the first is forced to be surjective, and a
surjection `A/Iᵏ ↠ A/(f₀ⁿ, …, f_{r-1}ⁿ)` forces `Iᵏ ⊆ (f₀ⁿ, …, f_{r-1}ⁿ)`. A uniform shift
would therefore give a constant `c` with `I^(n+c) ⊆ (f₀ⁿ, …, f_{r-1}ⁿ)` for all large `n`.

In the polynomial ring `ℤ[x₀, …, x_{r-1}]` on the sequence `f_i = x_i` no such constant exists:
the monomial `x₀^(n-1) ⋯ x_{r-1}^(n-1)` lies in `I^{r(n-1)}` and not in `(x₀ⁿ, …, x_{r-1}ⁿ)`,
which is the computation the source performs, and for `r ≥ 2` the exponent `r(n-1)` outgrows
`n + c`. Hence for `r ≥ 2` the two systems are not strictly pro-isomorphic.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.koszulToAdic` — the canonical comparison of inverse
  systems from `A/(f₀^(n+1), …)` to `A/I^(n+1)`.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.monomialExponent` — the exponent vector all of whose
  entries equal a given natural number.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.monomialIdeal` — the ideal `(x₀, …, x_{r-1})` of
  `ℤ[x₀, …, x_{r-1}]`.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.diagonalMonomial` — the monomial
  `x₀^(n-1) ⋯ x_{r-1}^(n-1)`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.le_of_surjective_quotientMap`: a surjection of
  `A`-modules `A/I ↠ A/J` forces `I ≤ J`.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.proIso_koszulToAdic`: the two systems are isomorphic as
  pro-objects, the comparison acquiring a two-sided inverse at every level after passing to a
  later one.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.exists_uniform_pow_le_powIdeal_of_strictProSection`: one
  half of a strict pro-isomorphism between the adic system and the Koszul system of quotients
  produces a constant `c` and a level `N` with `I^(n+c+1) ≤ (f₀^(n+1), …, f_{r-1}^(n+1))` for
  all `n ≥ N`.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.not_annihilator_pow_mul_pred`: the source's computation,
  that `H⁰(Kₙ) = ℤ[x]/(x₀ⁿ, …, x_{r-1}ⁿ)` is not annihilated by `I^{r(n-1)}`.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.not_exists_uniform_pow_le_monomialPow`: no constant `c`
  has `I^(n+c) ⊆ (x₀ⁿ, …, x_{r-1}ⁿ)` for all large `n`, once `r ≥ 2`.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.not_strictProIso_monomial`: for `r ≥ 2` the adic system
  and the Koszul system of quotients of `ℤ[x₀, …, x_{r-1}]` are not strictly pro-isomorphic;
  indeed neither composite of a pair of strict pro-morphisms is eventually the identity.
* `Stacks.LocalCohomology.UniformityII.not_nonempty_iso_proObj_monomial`: the same statement
  read in `Stacks.LocalCohomology.StrictProCategory (ModuleCat _)` itself — the two inverse
  sequences are not isomorphic objects of the strict pro-category.  This uses that the
  presentation of the strict pro-category by generators and relations is full and faithful,
  `Stacks.LocalCohomology.nonempty_iso_iff_exists_rep`.

## Implementation notes

The adic system is `AdicCompletionTor.idealPowerQuotientModuleSystem`, whose value at `n` is
`A/I^(n+1)`, and the Koszul system of quotients is `Koszul.powQuotientSystem`, whose value at
`n` is `A/(f₀^(n+1), …, f_{r-1}^(n+1))`; both index the positive powers by `n ↦ n + 1`, so the
levels match. Importing `StacksFrontier.RingTheory.LocalCohomology.StrictProCategory` puts a
`Quiver` instance on `ℕᵒᵖ ⥤ C` whose arrows are the strict pro-representatives, so natural
transformations of inverse systems must be written `NatTrans X Y` rather than `X ⟶ Y`.

Non-membership of the diagonal monomial is read off its coefficient: every element of
`(x₀ⁿ, …, x_{r-1}ⁿ)` has vanishing coefficient at each exponent vector all of whose entries are
smaller than `n`.

